@charanjit-singh This should be possible with regular promise handling, albeit it's an annoying pattern to write in JavaScript.

Something like this should work:

```ts
/**
 * Given an array of promises, resolve with the first result that is truthy.
 * If no results are truthy, will resolve with `null` after all promises have
 * settled.
 */
const waitForAny = <TPromises extends Promise<any>[]>(
  promises: TPromises
): Promise<Awaited<TPromises[number]> | null> => {
  // Use `new Promise()` to give us access to `resolve` and `reject` functions
  return new Promise(async (resolve, reject) => {
    // Chain logic to each promise that will call our outer `resolve` or
    // `reject` if we get a satisfactory result.
    const wrappedPromises = promises.map((p) => {
      return p
        .then((result) => {
          if (result) {
            resolve(result);
          }
        })
        .catch(reject);
    });

    // While that's happening, wait for everything to settle.
    await Promise.all(wrappedPromises);

    // This will always be called, but ignored if the `resolve` was called
    // above.
    resolve(null);
  });
};
```

You could use it like so:

```ts
