Yes. A function can have both cron and event triggers. Pass them together in a `triggers` array (up to 10 unique triggers) [1]:

```ts
inngest.createFunction(
  {
    id: "resync-user-data",
    triggers: [
      { event: "user.created" },
      { cron: "0 5 * * *" },
    ],
  },
  async ({ event, step }) => { /* ... */ },
);
```

The developer probably hit an error because they used the older single-trigger form, where the second argument takes only one `{ event }` or `{ cron }` [6]. Multi-trigger support was added later as an array of triggers [9]. They should use a recent SDK version and put everything in `triggers`.

In the handler, use `event.name` to tell which trigger fired. Cron runs arrive as `inngest/scheduled.timer`, and the `event` type is a discriminated union you can narrow on [3][9]:

```ts
if (event.name === "inngest/scheduled.timer") { /* cron run */ }
```

If they still get an error, ask for the SDK version and the exact error message.

